{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED — independent review of exact head 263068a79cde12171e015d397c0753852856b2b9 on request/succession-staleness, for review request f6878966 under governing request be558440, co-signing all four named artifacts: internal/workroom 5b673fdc0edaf6659171d74fd89486c2bfade9ec (primary), internal/docset dd2dac1da04573a4ce28643394e66d3d47e5d7a4, docs 2ae7a20da3c99adf13e3bb1606c7aa344350e7be and ui 75329c54f9bf9954d618f1a89386802b79814b12.\n\nThis replaces my report 681ee8de, whose judgement is unchanged and repeated in full below. That report rested only on the promise and the primary artifact while the head changes four maintained paths, and named the other three by eight-character prefixes. The request asked for a verdict naming all four, and an approval is read by the merge preflight, so a partial artifact set is what bounds a merge wrongly. I made the same filing error on the kernel head minutes earlier and corrected it there as 4551f32e; correcting it here too rather than leaving one right and one wrong.\n\nCODEX'S FINDING a9564eb5 IS CLOSED, and I checked each half. Succession now carries the link rather than a boolean: succeededRetirements returns a predecessor-to-successor map, so staleness follows the chain instead of stopping at the first hop. successionCondemned walks that chain to its end and reports the terminal artifact's retirement, with a visited set that returns false on a cycle — the conservative direction, declining to condemn rather than flaring on a malformed chain. A successor replaced by a further successor keeps answering; a terminal successor retired with no successor of its own condemns the predecessor, and what stood on it is told exactly as if its own basis had been retired. The merge receipt is included through withoutCondemnedSuccessions, so a signed plan stops covering a condemned chain.\n\nMUTATION. Forcing successionCondemned to return false fails TestCondemnedSuccessorFlaresWhatStoodOnThePredecessor and nothing else. The condemnation path is pinned by exactly the test the request named, and the worktree restored clean.\n\nTHE RULE NOW HAS ONE DEFINITION, the real simplification here. internal/docset/succession.go and its test are deleted, and the two gate call sites read artifact.Succeeded from the projection instead of recomputing the rule locally. I compared the two implementations before accepting the deletion: pathCovers is preserved in the fold with identical semantics — exact string, or a directory containing the predecessor, compared slash-delimited — so the docset gate classifies citations as it did, now from the fold's answer rather than a parallel copy of the reasoning. Two implementations of one rule is the shape that drifts, and this removes the second.\n\nSECURITY, AND THE CHOICE I MOST WANTED TO CHECK. Succession is established only by the retiring act's own signed rests_on naming an artifact at the same path or a covering directory. The code rejects the structural shortcut explicitly, and the comment names the failure it would cause: an artifact retired because its claim was never true would be quietly rescued by the next unrelated publication in that tree, and everything resting on the false claim would stop flaring. A bystander cannot supply the signed basis, so forging succession needs the retiring actor's own signature, the same authority the retirement already required. That is the right boundary, and it is the difference between a pointer that moved and a claim that was withdrawn. No unresolved security defect.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Four maintained paths move together and each carries its artifact at this head. internal/workroom changes the fold contract — Artifact.Succeeded is added and the schema version moves to workroom-fold@6 — and internal/docset loses a surface it no longer owns. The contract change is documented in the same head: docs/concepts/staleness.md, docs/reference/architecture.md and docs/reference/gs/status.md all state the distinction between a succeeded retirement and a condemnation, and ui/src/lib/api.ts mirrors the new field so the interface reads what the fold now publishes.\n\nGATES, INDEPENDENTLY RUN at the exact head in my own worktree: gofmt reports nothing, go vet is clean, git diff --check is clean, go test -count=1 ./... passes 30 packages with zero failures, go test -race is clean for internal/workroom, internal/wireparity and internal/statusview, and make ui-check exits 0 leaving the tree clean, so the committed embed matches ui/src.\n\nONE FACT ABOUT THE BASE, recorded rather than held against the head. This branch was cut from d6843b48 and main has since moved to eb7d2dfd, so it does not fast-forward; the request says so itself. It merges cleanly — git merge-tree against current main returns no conflict — and the branch's own three-dot diff is confined to twelve files. A two-dot diff against main will show files main gained since as deletions; that is base drift, not a revert, and whoever merges should read three-dot.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Already taken: this head deletes 152 lines while adding the behaviour, and the deletion is the duplicate rule rather than coverage. Nothing further to cut.\n\nNo change to this head is required. Any change to it invalidates this approval.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:5b673fdc0edaf6659171d74fd89486c2bfade9ec","artifacts":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:5b673fdc0edaf6659171d74fd89486c2bfade9ec, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:dd2dac1da04573a4ce28643394e66d3d47e5d7a4, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:2ae7a20da3c99adf13e3bb1606c7aa344350e7be, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:75329c54f9bf9954d618f1a89386802b79814b12","head":"263068a79cde12171e015d397c0753852856b2b9","verdict":"approved"}}